Here are the main issues I see in the original routine, and some suggestions on how to clean them up:

1) Serial each-ID DELETE  
   • Problem: you are calling  
       for (const id of ids) {  
         await db.query('DELETE …', [id])  
       }  
     so you pay the cost of a round-trip for each single id, one after the other.  
   • Fix: bundle them into one SQL DELETE. In Postgres you can do  
       DELETE 
         FROM records 
        WHERE id = ANY($1)  
     and pass the entire JavaScript array as parameter. That drives just one round-trip and lets the database do the set‐based delete in one shot.

2) Inaccurate “deleted” count  
   • Problem: you return `{ deleted: ids.length }` even if some of the IDs didn’t exist (or were duplicates).  
   • Fix: use the driver’s `.rowCount` (or `RETURNING`) to see how many rows actually got deleted:  
       const result = await db.query('DELETE … RETURNING id', [ids]);  
       return { deleted: result.rowCount, deletedIds: result.rows.map(r=>r.id) };

3) No transaction / partial failure  
   • Problem: if you really did want to do one‐by‐one deletes you could end up in a half-deleted state if one of them fails.  
   • Fix: always wrap multi-step operations in a BEGIN/COMMIT (and ROLLBACK on error) so that you either delete them all or none.

4) Lack of input validation & DoS vector  
   • Problem: you blindly trust `ids` is an array of acceptable primary-key values. A malicious client could pass an extremely large array and DOS your database, or even pass strings.  
   • Fix:  
     – Make sure `ids` is a non-empty array (or early return if it’s empty).  
     – Enforce a maximum length (`ids.length <= MAX_DELETE_BATCH`).  
     – Validate each element (e.g. `Number.isInteger(id)` or a UUID‐regex, depending on your PK).  
     – If validation fails, throw a 4xx error without hitting the DB.

5) Missing authorization check  
   • Problem: as written, any caller who can invoke `batchDelete([1,2,3])` could wipe out other users’ data if your table has no per-user filter.  
   • Fix: add a `user_id = $2` (or a JOIN) so that you only delete rows owned by the current user/session.

Putting it all together, here’s an example of a more robust implementation (Postgres + node-pg style):

  const MAX_DELETE_BATCH = 1000;

  async function batchDelete(ids, currentUserId) {
    // 1) Basic input validation
    if (!Array.isArray(ids) || ids.length === 0) {
      throw new BadRequestError('`ids` must be a non-empty array');
    }
    if (ids.length > MAX_DELETE_BATCH) {
      throw new BadRequestError(`Can delete up to ${MAX_DELETE_BATCH} records at once`);
    }
    // 2) Validate each id’s type
    for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) {
      if (!Number.isInteger(ids[i])) {
        throw new BadRequestError(`Invalid id at index ${i}: ${ids[i]}`);
      }
    }

    const client = await db.connect();
    try {
      await client.query('BEGIN');
      // 3) Optional ownership check
      const check = await client.query(
        `SELECT id 
           FROM records 
          WHERE id = ANY($1) 
            AND user_id = $2`,
        [ids, currentUserId]
      );
      if (check.rowCount !== ids.length) {
        throw new ForbiddenError('One or more records not found or not owned by you');
      }

      // 4) Perform the batch delete in one SQL round‐trip
      const del = await client.query(
        `DELETE 
           FROM records 
          WHERE id = ANY($1) 
            AND user_id = $2
        RETURNING id`,
        [ids, currentUserId]
      );
      await client.query('COMMIT');

      // 5) Return the real deleted count & list
      return {
        deleted: del.rowCount,
        deletedIds: del.rows.map(r => r.id),
      };
    } catch (err) {
      await client.query('ROLLBACK');
      throw err;
    } finally {
      client.release();
    }
  }

Key take-aways:  
• Do set-based deletes with `ANY($1)` or `IN (…)` instead of looping.  
• Validate your inputs (type, size).  
• Guard against partial failures with transactions.  
• Always check rowCount or use `RETURNING` so your API reports the real outcome.  
• Verify that the current user is allowed to delete each record.
